Dotkit — master build prompt for Google AI Studio
Build mode · phased · v1. Paste the whole prompt, then say “next” after each phase.
0. Mission
You are a principal product designer and senior full-stack engineer. Build Dotkit (working name — keep it in one APP_NAME constant): one installable, offline-first PWA that bundles hundreds of real, working tools in 22 chapters — downloaders, URL safety, local servers, an image lab, a PDF lab, calculators, finance, measuring tools, device tests, games, health, developer tools, brain training and Gemini-powered tools — behind one typographically led pixel interface.
Non-negotiables:
1.  Everything that ships works. No mock data, no fake progress bars, no dead buttons, no lorem ipsum. If a browser or the platform can’t do something, say so inside the tool, say why, and offer the best honest alternative (§6). Never fabricate sensor, network or device readings.
2.  One design language everywhere (§3): games, charts, canvases, PDF viewers, third-party widgets and error pages included.
3.  Private by default. Processing happens on the device. Anything that leaves it (Gemini, third-party APIs, the companion server) is labeled in the UI before it happens.
4.  It must not look AI-generated (§3.12).
5.  Phased delivery (§8). Never try to produce the whole app in one response.
Reference projects (open source; I confirm you may study them and port logic and feature lists freely — keep license headers and credit them):
• Seal — https://github.com/junkfood02/Seal (yt-dlp downloader UI)
• shttps — https://github.com/truefedex/shttps (HTTP/HTTPS server)
• ImageToolbox — https://github.com/T8RIN/ImageToolbox (image toolkit)
• PaperKnife — https://github.com/potatameister/PaperKnife (PDF toolkit)
If you can’t read a repo, treat the checklists in §7 as the minimum and also implement every feature you know those apps have.
1. Build protocol
• Build only the phase I name. First reply: a 10-line plan, then Phase 0 only. End every reply with a short report (done · deferred · known limits · next phase) and stop until I say “next”.
• If a phase doesn’t fit in one response, stop at a clean file boundary, list exactly which files remain, and continue when I say “continue”. Never truncate a file silently.
• Plugin architecture: adding a tool = one folder + one registry entry. Don’t rewrite shell or design-system files to add tools unless fixing a bug.
• Keep living docs in the repo and re-read them before every phase: DESIGN.md (tokens, components, motion, voice), ARCHITECTURE.md, ROADMAP.md (every tool in §7 with its status), CHANGELOG.md, DECISIONS.md (assumptions). Don’t ask me questions unless you’re blocked.
• TypeScript strict, files ≤ 400 lines. Pure logic lives in src/lib/** with Vitest tests for calculators, converters, parsers, date math and URL heuristics.
• Every tool ships with loading, empty, error, success and offline states; keyboard operation; screen-reader labels; undo for destructive actions; remembered last settings; copy/save/share for outputs; and a one-paragraph help sheet.
• Prefer MIT/Apache/BSD dependencies. Flag any GPL/AGPL dependency, keep it out of the client bundle where possible, and add an Open-source Licenses screen crediting every project and library (name, license, link).
2. Architecture
2.1 Stack
React + TypeScript + Vite (AI Studio’s scaffold). Tailwind only as a utility layer mapped to our CSS variables — never Tailwind’s default palette. Zustand for state; Dexie (IndexedDB) and OPFS for storage; motion (formerly framer-motion) for layout and spring animation; Web Workers for anything over ~16 ms; ONNX Runtime Web (WebGPU when present); Workbox for offline. Gemini via @google/genai: read the key from the environment variable AI Studio injects, never hard-code it, and let the user override it in Settings → AI. Keep every model name in src/lib/ai/models.ts: newest Flash-class model for text, newest Gemini image model for generation and editing, Gemini TTS for speech, the Live API for voice. Don’t hard-code outdated names.
2.2 Layout
src/
  app/        shell, router, command bar, settings, onboarding
  design/     tokens.css, fonts, components/, icons/, motion/, specimen/
  registry/   manifest generated from tools/**/meta.ts
  tools/<chapter>/<tool-id>/   meta.ts · index.tsx · worker.ts? · lib/
  lib/        pure logic + tests (math, dates, url, crypto, units…)
  services/   gemini/ companion/ capabilities/ storage/ notifications/
server/       companion server (Node + TypeScript, own package.json)
2.3 Registry and tool contract
Every tool exports meta.ts: id, number, chapter, name, tagline, keywords[], aliases[] (any language), requires[] (camera · mic · motion · geo · bluetooth · companion · ai · network · webgpu · fs-access · clipboard), offline: boolean, io: { accepts[], produces[] } (kinds like image/*, pdf, audio/*, video/*, text, url, json, file), status: live | planned. The Home screen, Index, search, command bar, “Send to…” menu, PWA shortcuts and a hidden Roadmap view are all generated from the registry. planned tools are hidden from users.
2.4 Capability layer
useCapabilities() probes once and caches: camera/mic, DeviceMotion/Orientation, Generic Sensors, Geolocation, Battery, Network Information, Web Bluetooth/USB, Web Share, File System Access, EyeDropper, BarcodeDetector, Compute Pressure, WebGL/WebGPU, SharedArrayBuffer, Notifications, Clipboard, Vibration, Wake Lock, Speech APIs and companion health. Tools show requirement chips; when something is missing they say exactly what, why and what to do (iOS motion/orientation permission needs a user gesture — handle it).
2.5 Storage
Dexie tables per domain (settings, history, favorites, notes, tasks, expenses, vault, jobs, servers…); OPFS for files. A Files screen lists every output (thumbnail, size, origin tool; save, share, send to, delete). Full backup/restore to an encrypted .dotkit file (AES-GCM, passphrase). The Password Vault has its own key hierarchy (Argon2id via WASM or PBKDF2 ≥ 600k iterations, AES-GCM, auto-lock, clipboard auto-clear).
2.6 Companion server (/server)
Node 20+ with TypeScript (Hono or Express), shipped with a Dockerfile and docker-compose that bundle yt-dlp, ffmpeg, aria2c, gallery-dl and the JavaScript runtime yt-dlp needs for YouTube (follow the yt-dlp wiki for the current requirement; the setup check must report it), plus a one-line local run script. The web app talks to it through services/companion (URL + bearer token in Settings; same-origin /api by default). If it’s unreachable, tools that need it show a “Companion required” panel with setup steps and a Test connection button; every other tool keeps working.
Modules: downloads, music, gallery, url-trace, tls, screenshots (isolated headless browser, JavaScript off by default), servers, net (ping, TCP, traceroute, DNS), speed-test endpoints, ffmpeg offload, web push, webhook inbox, P2P signaling.
Security rules (mandatory): an SSRF guard on every outbound fetch — resolve DNS first; block loopback, link-local, private, CGNAT, multicast and cloud-metadata ranges; re-check on every redirect hop; cap hops (≤ 15), time, bytes and content types. Spawn processes with argv arrays, never shell strings; allow-list yt-dlp flags in the raw-args field. Token auth plus a CORS allow-list; per-IP rate limits; sandboxed temp directories with quotas and auto-cleanup; never execute user code on the server; redact cookies and tokens in logs.
2.7 iframe and hosting notes
AI Studio previews run inside an iframe: declare every permission the app needs in metadata.json (camera, microphone, geolocation, clipboard… where supported) and show an “open in a new tab / install” hint for features iframes block (sensors, Bluetooth, fullscreen, wake lock). The PWA must install and work offline after the first load (precache the shell and design assets; lazily cache tool chunks and WASM). Set COOP/COEP headers where hosting allows so ffmpeg.wasm can use threads; otherwise fall back to single-thread automatically.
3. Design language: “Pixel Editorial”
3.1 Concept and principles
A field guide set by a typographer, printed on a pixel grid. Every tool is a numbered specimen (№ 001 and up) in a catalogue that reads like a type-foundry book: huge pixel-display numerals and titles, hairline rules, small mono labels, tabular figures and one flat, saturated colour per chapter. The modern part lives in behaviour: variable fonts, OKLCH colour, spring physics, view transitions, container queries, haptics and a true dark theme.
1.  Type is the interface. Icons support words; they never replace them.
2.  One grid. Everything snaps to a 4 px unit; pixel art only ever scales by whole numbers.
3.  Flat colour, hard edges. No gradients, blur, glow, glass or soft shadows. Depth comes from paper layers, 2 px ink lines, hard offset shadows and dither.
4.  Colour is wayfinding. Each chapter owns a hue; state colours (ok, warn, error, info) mean one thing everywhere.
5.  Motion explains cause and effect, stays under 400 ms and respects reduced motion.
6.  Every state is designed: loading, empty, error, offline, success.
3.2 Type system
Five roles, all SIL OFL, self-hosted as subsetted woff2 (@fontsource packages or /public/fonts), preloaded, with metric-matched fallbacks (size-adjust) so nothing jumps. Never more than three families on one screen.
• Display (pixel): Pixelify Sans for chapter titles, tool names and big numerals. Set it at whole multiples of its pixel grid (16 / 24 / 32 / 48 / 64 / 96 / 128 px), with no fractional letter-spacing and -webkit-font-smoothing: none where it works, so stems stay crisp.
• Readout (dot-matrix): Doto for clocks, counters, scoreboards and meter readouts.
• Text and UI: Bricolage Grotesque (variable: wght, wdth, opsz) for body, buttons and forms. Use the width and optical-size axes for voice: narrow and heavy for editorial headlines, normal width for reading.
• Data and labels: Martian Mono for metadata, tables, code and tags; labels in uppercase with 0.06em tracking.
• Accent serif (sparingly): Newsreader italic for help sheets, pull-quotes and empty states.
If a face can’t be loaded, choose another OFL face with the same role and record it in DESIGN.md. Scale: 12 / 14 / 16 / 20 / 24 / 32 / 40 / 56 / 72 / 96 / 128 px, with fluid clamp() on the top four. Line-heights are multiples of 4 px. Use font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums on every number, text-wrap: balance on headings and pretty on paragraphs, a measure ≤ 66ch, real quotes, dashes and ellipses, hanging punctuation, no faux bold or italic (load only the weights used), and hand-tuned kerning on display titles.
3.3 Grid, geometry and the pixel rule
• Unit: --u: 4px. Spacing, sizes and corner steps are multiples of it. Columns: 4 / 8 / 12 with 16 / 24 px gutters; content ≤ 1200 px; reading column 66ch.
• Lines: hairline 1 px (table rules), strong 2 px (borders), heavy 4 px (chapter headers).
• Corners: no border-radius. Use stepped “bitmap” corners (one or two 4 px steps) from one shared PixelBox mask used by buttons, inputs, cards, dialogs, tooltips and canvas frames.
• Shadows: a hard offset “ink shadow” (4px 4px 0 var(--ink)); pressing moves the element into its shadow. Never a blur radius.
• Layers: paper → paper-2 (cards) → paper-3 (wells). Modals use a dither scrim (2×2 checker at 40%), not blur.
• Dither: ship Bayer 4×4 patterns (.dither-25 / 50 / 75) as CSS or SVG masks for disabled states, skeletons, progress tracks, image placeholders and chapter patterns.
• Images: pixel art scales by integer factors with image-rendering: pixelated; photos scale smoothly but sit in a crisp 2 px frame (a “plate”) with a numbered caption.
