3.5 Component kit
Build one kit and use it everywhere: buttons (solid in chapter colour, outline, ghost, destructive; 32 / 40 / 48 px; 44 px minimum touch target), switch, checkbox, radio, stepped slider (tick marks, block thumb), stepper, segmented control, index tabs (catalogue dividers), chips, inputs and textareas (2 px ink border on a paper-3 well, caret in the chapter hue), select, menu, popover, command palette, dialog (side panel on desktop, bottom sheet on phone), toast (ticker-tape stack), tooltip, progress (8-segment block bar, pixel spinner, marching blocks), skeleton (stepped dither shimmer), badge, specimen card (number, name, tagline, glyph, requirement chips, corner crop marks), tables (hairline rows, sticky header, tabular numerals), accordion, breadcrumb (“№ 047 / Image Lab / Resize”), dropzone (stepped marching ants), empty and error states (pixel scene, one line, one action, copyable details), key caps, gauges and dials, a chart kit (line, bar, step-area, pie and donut as pixel wedges, scatter, radar, heatmap, sparklines; flat fills plus dither; axes in Martian Mono), custom date, time and colour pickers (never native), an on-screen numeric and scientific keypad, a media player, and canvas-editor chrome (toolbar, layers, history, zoom with 1:1 pixel peek).
Third-party widgets (code editors, charts, PDF viewers, maps) must be fully re-skinned with these tokens or replaced. The same language covers everything a user can see: games, charts, canvases, PDF and media viewers, QR codes, the splash screen, 404 and offline pages, PWA and notification icons.
3.6 Icons and illustration
• Draw the icon set as 16×16 bitmaps stored as ASCII strings (# = ink, + = accent, . = empty) in design/icons/*.ts; a build step compiles them into one SVG sprite with shape-rendering: crispEdges. Render at 1×, 2× or 3× only; two tones at most.
• Start with the 22 chapter glyphs plus about 140 common glyphs. Every tool gets its own glyph, with a two-letter monogram tile (Pixelify Sans on the chapter colour) as the fallback. No stock icon packs, no emoji in the UI, no sparkle icons: AI features use the same glyph language and are labelled “Gemini”.
• Illustrations (empty, error, offline, onboarding) are ≤ 64×64 pixel scenes of “specimen objects”: pencil, ruler, loupe, floppy disk, cassette, dial, stamp. No mascots.
3.7 Motion
Tokens: --ease-out: cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1); stepped easing steps(8) for pixel effects; UI spring { stiffness: 420, damping: 34, mass: 0.8 }; page spring { stiffness: 260, damping: 30 }. Durations: micro 80–120 ms, small 160–220, panels 280–360, hero ≤ 600, never longer. Implement these signature moves and reuse them everywhere:
1.  Typed-on titles: display titles reveal in 8–12 pixel steps, once per screen per session.
2.  Shared-element transitions: specimen card → tool page through the View Transitions API (view-transition-name = tool id), with a motion layout-id fallback.
3.  Press physics: buttons drop into their hard shadow; optional 8 ms haptic tick.
4.  Dither wipe: route changes sweep a Bayer-pattern mask in the chapter colour (about 220 ms).
5.  Dot-matrix roll: readouts change digit by digit.
6.  Block progress: bars fill in discrete segments with a travelling highlight; indeterminate = marching blocks.
7.  Lists: staggered 8 px rise, 20–30 ms apart (cap at 12 items); FLIP reorder with a spring.
8.  Focus and hover: a 2 px focus ring with a 2 px gap in --focus, visible on both themes; hover nudges by one unit.
9.  Feedback: square-pixel confetti on success (toggle), a two-frame ±4 px shake on error.
10.  Ambient: static dither paper grain; optional drift at ≤ 2 fps, off in battery saver.
Animate only transform, opacity, clip-path or mask; target 60 fps; pause when the tab is hidden. Settings → Motion offers Full / Calm / Off and defaults to the OS setting. Reduced motion replaces everything with fades of ≤ 120 ms: no stepped typing, no shakes. Optional UI sound (tiny WebAudio blips) is off by default and has its own switch.
3.8 Layout patterns
Phone: a bottom bar (Home · Index · Search · Files · You) and a horizontally scrolling chapter ribbon, with thumb-reachable primary actions and safe-area insets. Desktop: a left chapter rail, a top command bar and the content. A tool page has a header (№, name, tagline, chapter chip, requirement chips), a bench (the work area), a tray (options: bottom sheet on phone, right panel on desktop) and an output bar (Save · Share · Copy · Send to…). Sensor tools get landscape layouts; games, clocks and level and compass tools go truly fullscreen. Break the grid on purpose: big numerals bleeding off the edge, margin notes, rotated labels, ruler ticks along edges, fold lines and crop marks on cards.
3.9 Voice and microcopy
Short, concrete, dry. Sentence case. Buttons are verbs (“Resize”, “Save as PNG”, “Start server”). Errors say what happened, why and what to do next: no “Oops”, no exclamation marks outside games, no “seamless”, “unlock” or “supercharge”. Always show units and locale-correct numbers (en-IN grouping, 12,34,567, by default; region switchable).
3.10 Accessibility inside the design
WCAG 2.2 AA; visible focus; 44 px targets; every slider, dial and canvas control has a numeric or keyboard equivalent; ARIA for custom widgets; colour-blind-safe chart sets with pattern fills; text scale 85–150%; a high-contrast theme; an optional dyslexia-friendly UI face (Atkinson Hyperlegible) that replaces Bricolage; logical CSS properties so RTL works; photosensitivity warnings with a hard cap and a confirm step on strobe and flash features.
3.11 Customisation (Settings → Look)
Theme (Paper / Ink / Auto), accent, motion, UI scale in whole steps (1× / 1.5× / 2×), density (cozy / compact), type (standard / dyslexia-friendly), sound, haptics. The defaults must already look finished.
3.12 The “not AI-generated” gate
Never: purple or blue-violet gradients; glassmorphism, blur or glow; rounded-2xl card grids with emoji icons; Inter, Roboto or system-ui as the visible voice; Lucide, Heroicons or Material icons; a centred hero with a gradient headline and two buttons; three identical feature cards; sparkles; the default shadcn or Tailwind palette; “Welcome to…” copy; lorem ipsum or stock avatars; neon-on-black “cyber” styling; grids of identical cards with no hierarchy.
Always: asymmetric editorial layouts; extreme scale contrast (a 96 px pixel numeral beside a 12 px mono label); catalogue numbering; registration marks, ruler ticks, stamps (“DONE”, “OFFLINE”) and perforations on tear-off outputs; real content in every state; and one signature visual per chapter (Fetch: cassette-reel progress; Time: dot-matrix faces; Measure: ticked tape rules; Serve: patch-cord ports with status LEDs; Image Lab: contact sheet and loupe; PDF Lab: folded-page corners; Play: arcade scoreboard in Doto; Body: a pixel pulse line; Mind Lab: stopwatch-and-grid lab sheet; and so on).
Design QA gate: build /specimen in Phase 0 (type scale, colours in both themes, every component and state, the icon sheet, motion demos). No phase is “done” until its tools pass this gate at 375, 768 and 1280 px, in both themes, with reduced motion on and off, and I have said so.
4. Product shell
4.1 Home: a table of contents
Top: wordmark and command-bar trigger. Then Continue (recent tools and unfinished jobs), Pinned (drag to reorder) and Today (suggestions from recent use, time of day and pending jobs; clipboard-based suggestions only after I tap Paste). Below, the chapters as a typographic contents page: huge numerals, chapter name, tool count, leader dots. Tapping a chapter expands its specimen cards: one featured card, the rest as lines, never a wall of identical tiles.
4.2 Index and command bar
The Index lists every tool A–Z with filters (works offline · needs camera, mic or sensors · needs companion · uses Gemini). The command bar (⌘/Ctrl-K, /, or pull-down on phones) fuzzy-searches names, keywords and aliases in any language, and also answers inline: “5 km in miles”, “18% of 2400”, “emi 5L 8.5% 5y”, “time in tokyo”, “uuid”, “md5 hello”. Results preview inline and open the tool pre-filled. A pasted URL offers Fetch, Link Check and Unshorten; a dropped file offers Send to… filtered by type; a colour code offers the colour tools. Unmatched text becomes “Ask Gemini” when AI is enabled.
4.3 Intents and installation
PWA manifest with share_target (text, URL, files), file_handlers (PDF, images, audio, video, zip, where supported), shortcuts (top four plus pinned), a maskable pixel icon, a single-instance launch handler, standalone display and a theme colour that follows the active theme. A clipboard prompt appears on focus (“Link on clipboard: check or download?”) and never reads silently.
4.4 Send to… and Chains
Every output offers Send to…, listing tools whose io.accepts matches. Chains save multi-step recipes (Fetch → Trim → Convert → Tag → Save; Scan → Crop → OCR → PDF) that run in the Jobs queue; chains export and import as JSON or QR. The tool contract in §2.3 gains alsoIn[]: a tool lives in one chapter but can be listed in others (Common and Essentials are mostly alsoIn entries).
4.5 Library screens
Files (§2.5), History (per tool, searchable, clearable), Jobs (queue with pause, cancel, retry), Favourites and Collections (“Travel”, “Work”), Offline manager (WASM packs and AI models with sizes, installed and deleted individually), Privacy dashboard (network ledger per tool, permission states, data inventory, export-all, delete-all) and Licences.
4.6 Settings
Look (§3.11) · Language and region (English first; i18n- and RTL-ready; en-IN default for number grouping, INR and DD/MM) · Companion (URL, token, Test connection, engine versions) · AI (Gemini key override, models, consent, cost meter, “Offline only” kill switch) · Privacy · Notifications · Storage and backup · Accessibility · About.
4.7 First run
At most three skippable screens: what stays on the device, optional companion setup, optional Gemini key. Permissions are requested only at the moment of use, preceded by a one-line rationale sheet.
4.8 Offline and updates
Precache the shell and design assets; cache tool chunks lazily. Each tool shows an “offline” pill or the precise reason it can’t work offline. A non-blocking “New version: reload” toast appears when an update is ready; never auto-reload during a job.
5. Cross-cutting systems
5.1 Jobs
One queue for downloads, conversions, OCR, AI calls and servers. States: queued · running · paused · done · failed · cancelled. Concurrency limits; pause, resume, cancel and retry with backoff; determinate progress with ETA; logs; persistence across reloads (resume where possible); notifications with actions; an optional wake lock; battery-aware throttling. Anything over about 16 ms runs in a worker.
5.2 Gemini layer (services/gemini)
One client for everything: streaming, JSON-schema structured output, function calling, Google Search grounding, code execution, vision, audio understanding, TTS, the Live API, image generation and editing. Retries with jittered backoff, cancellation, caching of idempotent calls, a token and cost meter, and a global “Offline only” switch. Every AI tool shows a Gemini chip and asks for consent the first time it sends data off the device (what, and to whom). Files are never sent without a confirmation. The app must work fully without a key. Show sources only from real grounding metadata; never invent citations, prices, statistics or rankings. Factual outputs carry a “Check important details” footer.
5.3 Privacy
No analytics, telemetry or ads. A session network ledger lists every outbound request by tool, host and bytes, so local tools visibly show 0 B. A permission rationale appears before each browser prompt; every tool can reset its permissions; a Panic button wipes all Dotkit data. The companion’s net module has no port-range scanner or LAN sweeper: TCP checks are single-port, rate-limited and aimed at public hosts (or the user’s own machine, with confirmation).
5.4 Performance budgets
Shell ≤ 150 KB gzipped JS; each tool chunk ≤ 250 KB gzipped, excluding WASM and models; LCP < 2 s on a mid-range Android phone over 4G; interaction latency < 100 ms; Lighthouse PWA, performance and accessibility ≥ 90. Tile or stream anything over about 50 MP or 500 PDF pages; add memory guards that show a clear message instead of crashing; use WebGPU where present and WASM otherwise.
5.5 Testing
Vitest for src/lib/**; one Playwright smoke test per tool (opens, runs a built-in sample, no console errors, passes axe); screenshot tests of /specimen; fixtures for images, PDFs, audio and video. Each phase ends with a manual checklist I can run in five minutes.
5.6 Licensing and credits
Seal is GPL-3.0 and PaperKnife is AGPL-3.0, so copying their code would make Dotkit GPL or AGPL. Default: re-implement from their feature lists (features aren’t copyrightable) with permissively licensed libraries, and copy code only from Apache or MIT projects (ImageToolbox is Apache-2.0) with attribution, unless I record in DECISIONS.md that Dotkit may be AGPL-3.0. Check shttps’s LICENSE the same way. Seal’s licence also reserves its name: never use “Seal” for anything in the app. Keep a licence registry for libraries, models and datasets (name, version, licence, link), flag GPL, AGPL and non-commercial items (for example Stockfish, Ghostscript, mupdf, Essentia.js, RMBG, CodeFormer) and keep them out of the client bundle unless I approve.
6. Honesty table: what a browser can’t do
If a tool can’t do something on this device, it says so inside the tool, in plain words, and offers the nearest real alternative. Every reading is labelled with its source and its accuracy. Never fake data.
• Downloaders, music, URL trace, local servers, ping and traceroute, page screenshots, ffmpeg offload need the companion. Without it, show a “Companion required” panel with setup steps and a Test button; the rest of the app is unaffected.
• Streaming-service downloads (Spotify, Apple Music, Tidal, Amazon Music …): that audio is DRM-protected and Dotkit never rips or decrypts it. It reads track lists and matches each track to a source the user chooses (see Tunes); the first-run notice says that copyright and site terms still apply.
• DRM video (Widevine, FairPlay, PlayReady): detect and refuse, with an explanation.
• Root or jailbreak check: impossible from a web page. The tool becomes Integrity Check (what a website can actually see) and says why.
• CPU model, clock speed, temperatures, per-core load, battery temperature: not exposed. Show core count, architecture, a benchmark and the Compute Pressure state; the companion can report details of the host machine, labelled “Host”.
• Wi-Fi SSID, signal strength, channel; passive Bluetooth scanning: not exposed. Show Network Information (type, downlink, RTT) and the public IP via companion or API; Bluetooth becomes Bluetooth Explorer (pick a device, then read its GATT).
• Sensors (magnetometer, ambient light, barometer, proximity) vary by browser and often sit behind flags: probe, label “not available here”, give the flag or browser hint where one exists, and never simulate.
• Metal detector: magnetometer only; it detects magnetic fields, not gold, and is unavailable on many browsers.
• Sound and light meters are uncalibrated. Offer a calibration offset against a reference and label results “indicative, about ±5–10 dB” or “±20%”. Never for compliance or safety decisions.
• Heart rate (camera PPG), pedometer, sleep: estimates, “not a medical device”. The pedometer counts only while the page is open and awake. Support Bluetooth heart-rate straps for real accuracy.
• Alarms, reminders and timers when the app is closed: browsers throttle or kill background pages. Offer Web Push through the companion, “Add to calendar” (ICS) and a plain warning; never promise that a reminder will fire.
• Torch works in Chrome on Android only; other browsers get a screen light. iOS Safari lacks Web Bluetooth, vibration and the Battery API and gates motion sensors behind a permission: detect and explain.
• Status Downloader (WhatsApp statuses): Android hides that folder from browsers. The tool lets me pick files or a folder where the browser allows it and otherwise explains the manual route.
• Writing to the system gallery or saving in the background isn’t possible from a PWA; use Save As, Web Share or the Files screen.
• System clipboard history is impossible; Dotkit keeps an in-app history only.
• Opening PDFs, ZIPs, vaults or accounts without the password: refused. Unlock only with a known password.
• Code Runner runs client-side only (JS/TS, Python via Pyodide, SQL, Lua, Ruby, PHP in WASM). The companion never executes user code; a user-supplied external executor URL is allowed in Settings, clearly labelled.
• AI outputs show uncertainty. No medical, legal or financial conclusions; no symptom checker, no “brain age”, no fake percentiles or popularity numbers.
7. Tool catalogue
How to read it: every listed feature is a minimum that must exist and work by the end of its phase; “Also” items are quality-of-life extras. Where a reference app is named, match every feature it has (read the repo README if you can reach it), then exceed it. Tools marked (alias) live in another chapter and appear here through alsoIn. Keep IDs stable (fetch-video, img-resize …); the № numbers follow catalogue order. If anything here would break §1 or §6, tell me and propose the closest honest version.
01 Fetch: video and audio downloader (needs the companion)
