03 Link Check: URL safety and tracing (companion for deep checks)
Verdicts are layered, transparent and advisory. Link Check never says “100% safe”.
• Input: paste, QR scan (decode a QR before opening it), share-target, clipboard prompt, batch (CSV, pasted lists, or URLs extracted from text, HTML or email headers).
• Unshorten and trace: follow redirects on the companion (SSRF-guarded, ≤ 15 hops) and show every hop: status, Location, meta-refresh, JS redirects (optional headless render), timing, IP, ASN and country, TLS issuer, https→http downgrades; loop detection. Unwrap redirect wrappers (google.com/url, l.facebook.com, click trackers) and strip tracking parameters (utm_*, fbclid, gclid, igshid, si …) with a diff and a clean link. Without the companion: parsing and offline heuristics only, and say so.
• Layer 1, offline heuristics: punycode and IDN homographs, mixed scripts, confusables, look-alikes of a bundled top-sites list, suspicious TLDs, subdomain stuffing (paypal.com.evil.tld), IP-literal hosts, @ in the userinfo, long or high-entropy hostnames, risky schemes (data:, javascript:, file:), non-standard ports, double extensions, executable or archive downloads, free-hosting and dynamic-DNS domains.
• Layer 2, reputation (optional; user-supplied keys; every source labelled): Google Safe Browsing, VirusTotal, URLhaus, PhishTank and OpenPhish feeds (hash list kept on the companion), urlscan.io, AbuseIPDB, DNS blocklists (Spamhaus DBL, SURBL), family-DNS block checks (Cloudflare 1.1.1.2 and Quad9 over DoH), certificate-transparency history (crt.sh), RDAP domain age, popularity rank (a hashed top-N list such as Tranco, credited).
• Layer 3, technical profile: DNS (A, AAAA, MX, NS, TXT, CAA, DNSSEC), RDAP, TLS (issuer, validity, SANs, protocol, chain), HTTP headers (HSTS, CSP, redirects, cookie names), tech fingerprint, favicon hash, IP geolocation, an isolated headless screenshot (JavaScript off by default), page analysis (title, forms, password fields, external form actions, iframes, brand-impersonation hints), and file-type sniffing plus hashes for download links (never executed).
• Layer 4, Gemini second opinion (optional): structured JSON built from the findings and the visible page text (never cookies): “impersonating a known brand?”, reasons, confidence. Advisory only; Google Search grounding checks whether a domain is a brand’s official site.
• Verdict UI: one stamp (LOOKS OK · SUSPICIOUS · DANGEROUS · UNKNOWN) with an evidence list, a plain explanation per signal and a “checked by” list; copy, export (Markdown, JSON, PDF), defanged output (hxxps://).
• Also: history and a watchlist (re-check periodically; alert if a trusted link turns bad), allow and deny lists, “Open safely” (screenshot view, text-only reader, or copy defanged), auto-check on paste, an email-header analyser (SPF, DKIM, DMARC, Received chain, Reply-To mismatch), attachment hash check, scam-pattern hints for SMS text, region-aware “Report it” links (for India: cybercrime.gov.in), a link-rot checker.
• URL toolbox (offline): parser, builder and editor (scheme, userinfo, host, port, path, query table, fragment), encode and decode, normalise, punycode ↔ Unicode, UTM builder, deep-link and intent builder, compare two URLs, extract links from text, HTML or PDF, a short-link maker (companion SQLite with slugs, expiry and click counts).
04 Serve: local servers (SHTTPS-style; needs the companion to bind real ports)
SHTTPS (github.com/truefedex/shttps) is a simple HTTP server for sharing files and folders between devices and for prototyping small single-purpose sites. It ships as an Android app, a command-line server and a desktop GUI, built around its own small HTTP server library and a static web front end. Its README describes purpose and modules rather than listing features, so read the modules if you can reach them and match what they do, then deliver all of the following.
• Servers: many at once. Each has a name, a root folder (on the companion host, an uploaded workspace or a mounted volume), a port (auto or custom), a bind address (127.0.0.1, LAN or all), a protocol (HTTP, HTTPS, or both with redirect), HTTP/2, a themed directory listing (sortable, searchable, grid or list, “download folder as zip”), index files, SPA fallback, a custom 404, clean URLs, MIME overrides, gzip and brotli, Range requests, ETag and caching, a CORS preset editor, custom headers, rewrites and redirects, a reverse proxy to another local port (WebSockets included), basic or token auth, an IP allow-list, per-path rules, hidden-file and symlink policy, an upload endpoint (size and type limits), WebDAV (read-only or read-write), and connection and rate limits.
• HTTPS: a self-signed generator with SANs (localhost, LAN IPs, hostnames); a local CA (mkcert-style) that issues certificates and offers the CA for download with per-platform install steps (Android, iOS, Windows, macOS, Linux); PEM and PFX import; expiry reminders; TLS version and cipher choices; HSTS toggle; ALPN and H2; optional ACME for publicly reachable hosts.
• Sharing: URLs for localhost, LAN IPs and name.local (mDNS) with large QR codes; copy and Web Share; a connected-clients list with approve or deny; one-time and expiring links (by time or by downloads); a “share one file” quick mode (drag in, get a link, auto-stop after the first download); an optional tunnel through an installed cloudflared or ngrok-compatible binary, with a warning banner and mandatory auth; browser-to-browser P2P share (WebRTC) with no server at all.
• Dev extras: live reload, a mock API server (routes, delays, status codes, templated JSON, OpenAPI import), a request inspector, a request bin and webhook inbox, a record-and-replay proxy, network throttling profiles (3G, slow, flaky, offline), a CORS tester, “serve this Markdown or notes folder as a site”, a link-in-bio page export.
• Monitoring: live access log (tail and filter), requests per second and bytes sparklines, status LEDs, per-server uptime, auto-stop timers (“stop after 30 min”), auto-start with the companion, one Stop all button.
• Safety: default bind 127.0.0.1; LAN exposure needs an explicit toggle plus a persistent “reachable from other devices” banner; auth is suggested whenever LAN is on; path-traversal-proof; static files only (plus the mock engine); never execute served or uploaded code. Templates: Share a folder · Dev preview · Receive files · Mock API.
